Beryllium: a renderer starts the tracing thread after its lockdown

Purpose: the renderer lockdown (beryllium-capsicum-renderer.patch) sweeps
  every descriptor of the process and then enters capability mode. The
  sweep is complete only if no other thread can make or receive a
  descriptor meanwhile, and the lockdown has counted two threads in every
  renderer since it could count (threads=2, 0.20.3.15 and 0.20.3.16; the
  suite's gate fails on it).
  The other thread is Chromium's tracing thread. Initialising tracing
  (tracing::InitTracingPostFeatureList) constructs PerfettoTracedProcess,
  whose constructor starts a base::Thread named PerfettoTrace with an I/O
  message pump, whether or not anything is traced. Chromium knows this is
  wrong before a sandbox that needs a single thread: on Linux and ChromeOS
  ContentMainRunnerImpl::Run() only registers tracks early in a child
  process and sets needs_startup_tracing_after_sandbox_init, and
  RendererMain initialises tracing right after its sandbox call ("Startup
  tracing creates a tracing thread, which is incompatible on platforms
  that require single-threaded sandbox initialization"). The FreeBSD port
  added FreeBSD to that condition in RunZygote(), which FreeBSD never runs
  (it has no zygote), and not in ContentMainRunnerImpl::Run(), which it
  does: there the #else branch initialised tracing at once in every child
  process.
  An earlier reading of the ordering (0.20.3.15, in plan/14) took the
  RunZygote() block for the one FreeBSD runs and concluded that startup
  tracing waited for the sandbox. It did not. The kernel could not name
  the thread either: this port's PlatformThread::SetName() told only
  Chromium, so 0.20.3.16's thread_names read beryllium,beryllium
  (beryllium-bsd-kernel-thread-names.patch).
  This patch gives FreeBSD its own branch in Run(): a renderer defers
  tracing exactly as on Linux; every other process type starts it as
  before, having no lockdown yet. RendererMain's existing call after
  EnableSandbox() does the rest; it is not conditional on the platform.
  Starting the thread after cap_enter() needs a thread, a kqueue and an
  eventfd, as the I/O and compositor threads already do after the
  lockdown.
  What the thread could have done during the sweep: nothing was found. It
  has no Mojo connection before ChildThreadImpl exists, and its only
  self-started work, connecting to a system tracing socket, is off unless
  EnablePerfettoSystemTracing is switched on. The change is made to hold
  the invariant, not because a descriptor is known to have escaped.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Applies after the FreeBSD port's
  patch-content_app_content__main__runner__impl.cc (stage 2), which
  changes other hunks of the same file.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Compiled
  and released in 0.20.3.17 (10 November 2026): the owner's run of the
  sandbox tests with the lockdown on had threads=1 in four renderers, and
  lldb found one thread when RendererMain begins and one at the lockdown.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py: test_lockdown_log
  requires threads=1 at every lockdown when the lockdown is requested, and
  ThreadsAtLockdown lists the threads at that point with their stacks
  (lldb). Feature matrix row S-REN-01.
security-review: required (sandbox setup, process confinement)

--- a/content/app/content_main_runner_impl.cc
+++ b/content/app/content_main_runner_impl.cc
@@ -1218,6 +1218,23 @@
           needs_startup_tracing_after_sandbox_init = true;
           tracing::EnableEarlyTrackRegistration();
         }
+#elif BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
+        // Beryllium (plan/14-RENDERER-SANDBOX.md): initialising tracing
+        // starts the PerfettoTrace thread, and a renderer must be the only
+        // thread in its process when it locks down, so that the descriptor
+        // sweep there is complete. As on Linux, a renderer initialises
+        // tracing right after its sandbox call instead (RendererMain). The
+        // FreeBSD port made this change in RunZygote(), which FreeBSD never
+        // runs, and every renderer reached the lockdown with two threads.
+        // Other process types have no lockdown yet and start tracing here.
+        if (process_type == switches::kRendererProcess) {
+          needs_startup_tracing_after_sandbox_init = true;
+          tracing::EnableEarlyTrackRegistration();
+        } else {
+          tracing::InitTracingPostFeatureList(
+              /*enable_consumer=*/false,
+              /*will_trace_thread_restart=*/false);
+        }
 #else
         tracing::InitTracingPostFeatureList(
             /*enable_consumer=*/false,
